#: ../../docs/tutorials/inputs/controller_features.rst:4
msgid "Controller features"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/inputs/controller_features.rst:6
msgid "Godot supports controller-specific features that can further enhance the gameplay experience. This page describes these features, how existing games have used them, and how you can get started with them in Godot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/inputs/controller_features.rst:12
msgid "These controller features are currently only supported on Windows, macOS, iOS, and Linux."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/inputs/controller_features.rst:17
msgid "Unless you specifically advertise your game as requiring specific controllers, remember that there is no guarantee that players will have a controller with any given features."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/inputs/controller_features.rst:21
msgid "As a result, we suggest using these features to enhance the gameplay experience for players whose controllers support them, without detracting from those who don't have controllers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/inputs/controller_features.rst:27
msgid "LED color"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/inputs/controller_features.rst:29
msgid "Games can use the LED lights on certain controllers to subtly complement the on-screen gameplay by providing some matching visuals in the player's hands. Here are some notable examples:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/inputs/controller_features.rst:32
msgid "In *Hades*, the color of the light matches the god you're receiving a boon from."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/inputs/controller_features.rst:33
msgid "In *Resident Evil 2*, the color of the light indicates your health (green for full, yellow for medium, red for low)."
